====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|tree}} Any of various [[evergreen]] [[shrub]]s or [[tree]]s of [[genus]] {{taxfmt|Buxus|genus}}, especially [[common box]], {{vern|European box}}, or [[boxwood]] ({{taxfmt|Buxus sempervirens|species}}) which is often used for making [[hedge#Noun|hedges]] and [[topiary]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1587|author=Leuinus Lemnius|translator=Thomas Newton|title=An Herball to the Bible{{...}}|location=London|publisher=Edmund Bollifant|url=https://www.google.co.nz/books/edition/An_Herbal_for_the_Bible_Containing_a_pla/fKxkAAAAcAAJ|page=207|passage=And no maruell. For, the leaues of '''Boxe''' be [[deletorious]], poiſonous, deadlie, and to the bodie of man very noiſome, dangerous and peſtilent{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|volume=II|chapter=V|pages=130–131|pageref=130|passage=He strayed down a walk edged with '''box'''; with apple trees, pear trees, and cherry trees on one side, and a border on the other, full of all sorts of old-fashioned flowers, stocks, sweet-williams, primroses, pansies, mingled with southernwood, sweet-briar, and various fragrant herbs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ambra Edwards|title=Topiary: We're all going bonkers about box [print version: Bonkers about box, 22 November 2014, page&nbsp;G3]|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/plants/11238853/Topiary-were-all-going-bonkers-about-box.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Gardening)|date=19 November 2014|passage="'''Box''' makes a statement without having to do much: just trim twice a year and keep it weeded. It's a bit of a lazy gardener's plant." This, no doubt, is what makes '''box''' so popular with show home developers and city dwellers&nbsp;– there is scarce a balcony or front door anywhere that cannot be improved by a '''box''' ball in a pot.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251027012000/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/gardening/plants/11238853/Topiary-were-all-going-bonkers-about-box.html|archivedate=27 October 2025}}
# The [[wood]] from a box tree: [[boxwood]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John R. Jackson|title=Boxwood and Its Substitutes|journal=Journal of the Society of Arts|location=London|publisher=Published for the [[w:Royal Society of Arts|Society]] by [[w:George Bell (publisher)|George Bell and Sons]],{{nb...|4, 5, & 6, York Street, Covent Garden}}|date=10 April 1885|volume=XXXIII|issue=1,690|page=567|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/journalofsociety33soci_0#page/567/mode/1up|column=1|passage=Nevertheless, the application of woods other than '''box''' for purposes for which that wood is now used would tend to lessen the demand for '''box''', and thus might have an effect in lowering its price.}}
# {{lb|en|musical instrument|slang}} A [[musical instrument]], especially one made from boxwood.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Zora Neale Hurston|chapter=11|title=[[w:Their Eyes Were Watching God|Their Eyes were Watching God: A Novel]]|location=Philadelphia, Pa.; London|publisher=[[w:J. B. Lippincott & Co.|J.B. Lippincott Company]]|year=1937|oclc=144692287|edition=Harper Perennial Modern Classics|location2=Urbana, Ill.|edition2=Illini Books|publisher2=w:University of Illinois Press|year2=1978|page2=153|pageurl2=https://archive.org/stream/theireyeswerewat00hur_a4l#page/153/mode/1up|isbn2=978-0-252-00686-9|passage=Evenin’, folks. Thought y’all might lak uh lil music this evenin’ so Ah brought long mah '''box'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia}} An evergreen tree of the genus {{taxfmt|Lophostemon|genus}} (for example, {{vern|box scrub}}, [[Brisbane box]], {{vern|brush box}}, [[pink box]], or {{vern|Queensland box}}, {{taxlink|Lophostemon confertus|species}}).
# {{lb|en|Australia}} Various species of {{taxfmt|Eucalyptus|genus}} trees are popularly called various kinds of boxes, on the basis of the nature of their wood, bark, or appearance for example, {{vern|drooping box}} ({{taxlink|Eucalyptus bicolor|species}}), {{vern|shiny-leaved box}} ({{taxlink|Eucalyptus tereticornis|species}}), {{vern|black box|novern=1}}, or {{vern|ironbark box}} trees.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=J. H. Maiden|title=A Critical Revision of the Genus ''Eucalyptus''|year=1909|passage=The name ''"Black Box"'' seems to be most generally in use for this species, ''Eucalyptus boormani''; the even better name of "Ironbark ''Box''" (which certainly indicates its affinities) is nearly as frequently in use.|publisher=Government of the State of New South Wales}}

